      "name": "Data quality checklist: single exposure segment RWA",
      "description": "Required data fields for computing RWA on a single exposure segment, with rationale for each.",
      "content": "Required fields and why each matters:\n\n1. Exposure segment identifier — uniquely maps the exposure to the correct segment (bank, mdb, sovereign, corporate, retail, etc.) so the correct RWA approach (IRB vs Standardised) and exposure class apply.\n2. Exposure value (EAD) — after credit conversion factors (CCF) / off-balance-sheet adjustments; it is the base amount scaled by the risk weight, so any error directly scales RWA.\n3. Credit rating / grade — external (Standardised) or internal (IRB PD mapping); determines the risk weight (Standardised) or the PD bucket (IRB). Missing/invalid ratings must be flagged and handled (e.g., unrated treatment).\n4. Collateral / credit risk mitigation (CRM) — eligible collateral, guarantees, or credit derivatives reduce effective exposure or allow obligor risk-weight substitution; missing CRM data overstates RWA.\n5. Maturity — required where the maturity adjustment applies (IRB corporate/bank); affects the maturity factor in the RWA formula.\n6. Default status / days past due — determines defaulted/non-performing treatment (Standardised defaulted risk weight; IRB LGD considerations).\n7. Facility type / borrower characteristics — needed to select the correct sub-class and parameters, especially for retail and other granular segments.\n8. Segment-specific parameters (IRB) — LGD, maturity-adjusted PD, and correlation assumptions; flag segments where these cannot be reliably sourced.\n9. Currency — for consistent unit-of-measure and correct FX treatment where relevant.\n10. Validation flags — completeness, consistency, timeliness so missing or stale fields are surfaced rather than silently driving a wrong RWA.\n\nWhy each matters: every field feeds the risk-weight-to-RWA computation as either a base (EAD), a scalar (risk weight / PD), or an adjustment (collateral, maturity, default status), so a gap in any one propagates into the final RWA figure.",
      "content_type": "text/markdown"
    }
